+Reading from a file
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+You can display progress while reading a file with :func:`~rich.progress.open`. Pass a path (``str``, path-like object, or ``bytes``) and use the returned context manager in a ``with`` block. The default mode is text, so ``read()`` returns a ``str``. Use mode ``'rb'`` to receive ``bytes`` instead. The default total is the size of the file in bytes::
+
+    import rich.progress
+
+    with rich.progress.open("data.csv", description="Loading...") as f:
+        for line in f:
+            process(line)
+
+    with rich.progress.open("data.bin", "rb") as f:
+        data = f.read()
+
+Reading from a file-like object
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+If you already have an open binary file, use :func:`~rich.progress.wrap_file` with the total size in bytes. Only binary reads are supported. The wrapped file is not closed when the ``with`` block exits, so you still own it::
+
+    import rich.progress
+
+    with open("data.bin", "rb") as f:
+        with rich.progress.wrap_file(f, total=1024) as reader:
+            data = reader.read()
+
+Inside a :class:`~rich.progress.Progress` display you can attach a file to an existing task with :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.open` or :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file`. :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file` takes its total from the task when no ``total`` is given, and raises ``ValueError`` if the task has no total::
+
+    import rich.progress
+
+    with open("data.bin", "rb") as f:
+        with rich.progress.Progress() as progress:
+            task = progress.add_task("Loading", total=1024)
+            data = progress.wrap_file(f, task_id=task).read()
+
